        "body": "### What should we add?\n\nSome of this bears on #203, which is still open.\n\nI've been running the shipped pipeline on the [2Fe-2S] and [4Fe-4S] systems from the Sci. Adv. 2025 study and hit a defaults question the docs don't settle. Everything below comes from the standard configuration-recovery loop on 10⁶ samples drawn from a converged benchmark state.\n\n`symmetrize_spin=True` vs `False`:\n\n- ground-state energy difference: under 1 nanohartree, every batch of every iteration\n- subspace dimension: 4.00x larger (194,481 vs 48,600 determinants at iteration 4)\n\nSo on these systems it's pure cost. I don't think the implementation is wrong; as far as I can tell it does what it documents. Completion makes spin symmetry representable in the subspace, but nothing downstream selects for it, and in the ground manifold the S² block comes out proportional to the identity. It permits a singlet without ever producing one.\n\nThe package's own `spin_square()` on the result gives ⟨S²⟩ = 4.84 after 4 recovery iterations (3-batch mean; 4.83 on the best single batch). Target for these systems is a singlet at 0, so that's not a near miss; it isn't any pure spin state.\n\nMeanwhile `solve_sci` and `solve_sci_batch` both take a keyword-only `spin_sq` (default `None`) that feeds `fix_spin_(myci, ss=spin_sq)` and would target a spin sector directly. But `diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian` has no `spin_sq` parameter, and with `sci_solver=None` it calls the batch solver positionally:\n\n```python\nsci_solver(ci_strings, one_body_tensor, two_body_tensor, norb, nelec)\n```\n\nso `spin_sq` stays `None` on every default-path run. It's reachable through `functools.partial(solve_sci_batch, spin_sq=0.0)` and I've used it that way, so I'm not claiming anything is broken. What I can't tell is whether the pairing is deliberate: the spin option surfaced at top level doesn't target spin, and the one that does isn't surfaced.\n\nI've read #248, which asked for general `**kwargs` forwarding here and was closed as not planned, so I'm not re-litigating that. #308 asks for `shift` on `solve_sci`, which is the same `fix_spin_` family. Both are why I'm asking rather than opening a PR.\n\nTwo questions:\n\n1. Is `symmetrize_spin` meant to improve the spin character of the returned state, or only to make the symmetric sector representable? The docstring reads like the former to me and the measurement matches the latter. If it's the latter, I'm happy to send a docs PR saying so, with these numbers in it.\n2. Given #248, is `functools.partial(solve_sci_batch, spin_sq=...)` simply the intended way to target a spin sector from the top-level driver? A one-line \"yes, that's the pattern\" is a complete answer and I'll document it.\n\nOn #203: `open_shell` is still on `solve_fermion`, `optimize_orbitals` and `bitstring_matrix_to_ci_strs` (`fermion.py:543`, `:647`, `:795`) while the newer driver has moved to `symmetrize_spin`. Rather than add another opinion about the naming, treat the above as measurements of what the current default actually produces on a published benchmark.\n\nVersion: qiskit-addon-sqd 0.12.1. Full measurements, harness and raw data: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21359923, out of a spin audit of these benchmarks preprinted at https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv.15006382/v1. If I've misconfigured something, name the configuration and I'll rerun it and post the result here either way.\n\n- Tyler Vitale, Pure State Labs",
